WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.970
- [James] Consumer elasticities
have largely held up

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.670
better than expected year-to-date.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:07.950
- [Michele] We continue
to expect more elasticity.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.020
- [Narrator] Price
elasticity is a hot topic

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.360
in earnings calls during
periods of high inflation,

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:13.800
like the one we are in now.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.440
When inflation is high,
companies raise prices

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.020
and consumer spending usually slows.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.910
As you can see here, consumer spending

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.770
has held up relatively well so far,

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.140
despite the highest inflation in decades.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.720
But experts say we're finally
reaching a turning point

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.210
where consumer spending
is beginning to slow.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.730
And that's where
elasticity comes into play.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:34.770
Here's how price elasticity works

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.233
and why so many companies
are talking about it.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.010
- How much does demand
for something change

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.210
when the price changes?

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.350
When something's elastic,

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.660
it means if the price
goes up, demand falls.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.190
That's not a great thing for companies

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.170
because then they have to worry about that

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:51.750
any price increase will
really affect demand.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.370
- [Narrator] Here's the
chief commercial officer

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.710
of Southwest Airlines
talking about this idea

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.810
in the company's second
quarter earnings call.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.930
- [Andrew] Leisure travelers
have a price elasticity effect

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.413
where you can't go much higher.

00:01:02.413 --> 00:01:03.420
- Narrator] What companies want

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.430
is for their product to be in-elastic

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.790
meaning a price increase
doesn't really impact demand.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.190
Elastic products are
usually non-necessities,

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.140
like perfume and staples.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.810
In-elastic products are
typically things we really need

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:17.580
and that don't have many alternatives

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.410
like groceries and toilet paper.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:20.940
But there are some exceptions.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.470
- What has proven in-elastic,

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.512
particularly around the pandemic

00:01:24.512 --> 00:01:28.020
has been branded household goods.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.390
In past kind of bumpy economic times,

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.760
people have switched or traded down

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.650
to cheaper products around the house.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.240
A lot of people who
really watch the industry

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.210
have been surprised by how
much people are willing

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.670
to continue to spend on
these household basics.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.160
- [Narrator] Gym memberships
are also in-elastic right now.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.500
For example, Planet
Fitness raised the price

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.270
of its black card membership in May

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.220
bringing the monthly fee for new members

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.980
up from 22.99 to 24.99.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:54.630
And the company said in August...

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:55.800
- [Chris] We haven't seen an initial dip

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.630
in our black card percentage rate

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.910
as we did with the past price increases.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.170
- Covid has eased up.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.420
Everybody's kind of
making up for lost time.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.380
They're wanting to get out.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.610
So they're spending on travel.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.300
They're spending on experiences,
on movies, on eating out,

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.520
and it's been a really big shift

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.490
from goods to the service
side of the economy.

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.950
- [Narrator] Several factors
affects products' elasticity.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.780
- It's the necessity of the product.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.250
So how important is it?

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.480
You know, what's the competition like?

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:22.950
Are there a lot of other options?

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.450
Some of it's just emotional, you know.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:28.080
Some people really believe
a brand is a lot better

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.250
than another brand.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.590
- [Narrator] Elasticity also
depends on the price range

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:32.850
of the product itself.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.170
If it's a low cost item,

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.840
a slight increase in price
matters to consumers.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.310
If it's a more expensive item,

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:40.620
then a small increase isn't as noticeable.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:41.520
These are examples

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.940
of what executives consider
when they talk about elasticity.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:48.030
- What executives say and
think about elasticity

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:49.350
matters a lot to consumers

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.720
because ultimately their assessment

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.090
of what consumers are willing to pay

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.910
will drive their decisions
on how much to charge.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:59.280
Last year, when CEOS were
talking about elasticity,

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.850
they were more saying, wow,
like everything's in-elastic

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.400
because consumers are
just willing to spend.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.200
- [Narrator] Now, prices
are still going up

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.050
because of inflation but
demand is starting to go down.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.810
So certain products are
becoming more elastic.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.360
Unilever, Proctor and
Gamble, and Kraft Heinz

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.060
each reported lower volumes
in their latest quarters

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.190
meaning people purchase fewer brand items

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:21.630
amid higher prices.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:24.690
Many retailers have lowered
profit expectations for the year

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.810
and are cutting costs as consumers
are pulling back spending

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.090
in categories like apparel and home goods.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.340
- [Jeff] During the quarter,
Macy's brand customers

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.300
across all income tiers slowed
and shifted their spend.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.700
- [Narrator] In recent weeks,
sales of premium detergents,

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.260
like Tide, have been on the decline

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:41.640
according to industry data.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:42.900
- After all this time

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.510
of like sticking to this
very high-end detergent,

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:47.730
consumers are shifting
to cheaper detergent.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.500
- [Narrator] P&amp;G executives
said they believe

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.020
Americans will keep spending
on household products

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:55.239
even if inflation and the
overall economy worsen.

00:03:55.239 --> 00:03:56.072
(light music)

00:03:56.072 --> 00:03:58.500
So how can companies like P
and G maintain market share

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.720
when their products become more elastic?

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.540
One strategy is to promote
the cost saving benefits

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:04.380
of their products.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.170
- And it'll save you up to $150 a year.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:08.130
- And it's cold.

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.320
- There's a version of laundry detergent

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.210
that you can use in cold water.

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.610
So the detergent isn't less expensive.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:16.230
In fact, it's even more expensive

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:17.580
than it was six months ago.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.370
But they say, Hey, you know,
but the money you're gonna save

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:23.340
on not having to heat your
water makes this detergent

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.620
like overall more cost-effective.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:26.550
- [Narrator] Another strategy

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.280
is to offer smaller-sized
products at lower prices

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.230
to appeal to cash-strapped consumers.

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:33.611
- So if you only have
$10, you can still get

00:04:33.611 --> 00:04:35.730
your name brand detergent.

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.220
And that's something that
consumers who don't have

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:40.650
as much money do sometimes
out of necessity.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.350
It also means they're actually paying more

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.360
for each quantity of the product

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.820
because the less you buy,
the more expensive it is.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.800
- [Narrator] A P&amp;G
spokeswoman says the company

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.870
has always offered various package sizes

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:53.370
to meet different consumer needs.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:56.340
And those sizes vary
by retailer and market.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.290
Kraft Heinz takes a similar approach

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.030
by offering one-dollar Lunchables

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.640
and lower-priced packages
of Kraft Singles cheese

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:04.050
with fewer slices.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:06.660
Companies also have the
option to lean on retailers

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:08.010
to get more shelf space

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:11.210
or improve the presentation of
their products in the aisle.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.043
(light music)

00:05:12.043 --> 00:05:13.830
While inflation slowed a bit in August,

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:15.840
underlying price pressures and wage growth

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.720
suggest it could run well
above the Fed's 2% target

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:21.090
for some time, which
is why industry leaders

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.610
are keeping a close eye
on the labor market.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:26.190
The idea is that as long
as people stay employed,

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.740
consumer spending will
likely remain solid.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.200
But supply chain disruptions
and consumer demand

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.270
have both shown signs of easing recently

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:36.780
which economists expect will
slow price increases for goods.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.910
So the dilemma for many companies now

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:41.520
is whether to raise prices or cut costs.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.740
- When do we start laying off workers

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.180
so we don't have to raise prices?

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.220
Depending on the industry,
some companies are there,

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.570
some companies aren't there.

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.610
So I think figuring out when that happens

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:53.323
is where a lot of companies are right now.

00:05:53.323 --> 00:05:55.823
(light music)

